## THE CONTROL — is the second polarity actually load-bearing?

The claim "one polarity would not catch A1" is the spec's central premise, so it
was MEASURED, not assumed. Both pins were reduced to their `r_alt` half only
(the first-polarity assertions deleted), then A1 was applied:

    python3 single_polarity.py apply && python3 mutate.py A1
    npx vitest run src/ui/chain-builder-adapter.test.ts src/ui/AltCompare.test.tsx
    Test Files  2 passed (2)
    Tests  105 passed (105)

**A1 SURVIVES the single-polarity form** — a wholly wrong implementation, green.

Scrutiny (a surviving mutant can also mean a broken test): the SAME reduced pins
were re-run against A2, and they still kill it —

    python3 single_polarity.py apply && python3 mutate.py A2
    × marks the ALTERNATE row, whichever row is current (#116)
    × carries isAlternate from the RECIPE, not from the selection (#116)
    Tests  2 failed | 103 passed (105)

So the reduced pins are LIVE, not vacuous: they are specifically blind to A1, in
the exact way the correlation predicts. The second polarity is load-bearing.
Test files restored byte-for-byte from backup afterwards.

